doctors in California are trying to  solve a family stunning medical mystery  two young siblings four and six years  old were diagnosed with the same kind of  brain tumor within two weeks of each  other now their parents are speaking out  in the hopes of raising awareness and  encouraging new research Jamie yuccas  shows us the families brave fight I  think it's pretty rare to have two kids  who just are best friends  Kalea and Noah Avery have been bonded  from the beginning but their parents  never imagined the siblings would battle  cancer together it was right around  Memorial Day when six-year-old Kalia  started to experience severe headaches  and vomiting after repeated trips to the  emergency room an MRI revealed a tumor  when you heard a mass at the back of her  brain what went through your mind I  literally I mean I just I broke down  like fear the immediate reaction is fear  it's too hard to even say the words out  loud when you first get that diagnosis  you know in 14 days you have a girl who  loves a skateboard and play soccer and  hang out with their friends - she's got  that brain surgery and has brain cancer  like your whole world just stops less  than a week after Kalia had surgery to  remove her tumor her brother started  complaining about headaches when he said  you know my head hurts between my eye as  he's pointing right to where her sister  was we're like okay he's mimicking his  sister he misses his sister it's just  him dealing with all these emotions but  an MRI would reveal the same aggressive  tumor in the back of the brain known as  medulloblastoma the cancer affects  between 250 and 500 children in the US  every year I literally like I didn't  even respond I kind of went numb we're  all sitting hit with this and it's like  we're starting all over again with our  second child at that point I was a guy  how are we gonna get through this dr.  Allen Wayne at Children's Hospital Los  Angeles is treating the Avery children  they both had surgery already they're  doing well postoperatively  next comes beginning the chemotherapy  and in some cases additional therapy  Bianchi he and his team are also looking  for the cause of the cancer by studying  potential gene abnormalities it's  extremely rare to have two cancers in a  family which in and of itself raises the  possibility of this being an inherited  genetic predisposition what is the  likelihood that the cancer could come  back for these kids most of the time  with medulloblastoma the children are  cured but because the tumor affects the  cerebellum balance and coordination can  be affected the siblings are now working  with a team of oncologists and physical  therapists facing the road to recovery  together with the situation we're in  there's just you know different  victories there are different goals that  we have as a family and you know we look  forward to hitting those milestones when  they're like how you know how is Noah  doing her house Clea doing and you know  I'll say I she laughed for the first  time it's like I brings tears to my eyes  like just joy like the simplest things  are amazing now for CBS this morning  Jamie yuccas Los Angeles Jamie yuccas  thank you
 

